    {
      "id": "58689557d76031fee1c60305ad46d4a2614cbcc9",
      "individual_note": false,
      "resolvable": true,
      "resolved": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 1973962717,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Why not just clear the `WTAP_HAS_TS` flag for that record, so it's marked as not even *having* a time stamp?",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-06-28T19:42:22.151Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-01T08:01:20.441Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311673568,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16179,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1974530303,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Done. But now I have one concern: usually, there's always a metadata object in a BLF file, and having it a timestamp of 0, made `frame.time_relative` and `frame.time_relative_capture_start` the same. Now the information in the second field is completely gone when I convert the BLF to pcapng.",
          "author": {
            "id": 18219493,
            "username": "giovanni.musto",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Giovanni Musto",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b8ca1135be5e614246a54ed49c6809a9b172aafdcb27c2db7ec5548b625e76d5?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/giovanni.musto"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-06-29T16:18:47.082Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-01T08:01:20.441Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311673568,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16179,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1974761383,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> Now the information in the second field is completely gone when I convert the BLF to pcapng.\n\n*Neither* field should be present for a record/block that has no timestamp.  If a timestamp is being shown in the packet details, that's a bug.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-06-29T20:56:10.381Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-01T08:01:20.441Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311673568,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16179,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1974789824,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "This appears to be a bug in the re-reading of the capture file if you save it as a pcapng.  If you then close the current file and open the saved pcapng, `frame.time_relative_capture_start` is shown.\n\nIn any case, that's a bug having nothing to do with the BLF reader code.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-06-29T21:15:52.268Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-01T08:01:20.441Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311673568,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16179,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1974803571,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "The `frame.time_relative_capture_start` field was added in !9870.  It can only work for capture file formats that *have* a start time; pcapng currently only supports that as an option in [the Interface Statistics Block](https://ietf-opsawg-wg.github.io/draft-ietf-opsawg-pcap/draft-ietf-opsawg-pcapng.html#name-interface-statistics-block), which isn't necessarily present - and is likely to be at the *end* of the file.\n\npcapng probably needs to have a per-interface capture start time option.",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1b7c243a9b1d6587a9a9067e5d83aba1e87212df7c4eeae3690f791ecbe4675a?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/guyharris"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-06-29T21:27:49.511Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-07-01T08:01:20.441Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 311673568,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": true,
          "resolved": false,
          "resolved_by": null,
          "resolved_at": null,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 16179,
          "commands_changes": {}
        },
        {
          "id": 1976023510,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "> If you then close the current file and open the saved pcapng, `frame.time_relative_capture_start` is shown.\n\nAre you sure about that? That's not what I'm seeing.\n\n> pcapng currently only supports that as an option in [the Interface Statistics Block](https://ietf-opsawg-wg.github.io/draft-ietf-opsawg-pcap/draft-ietf-opsawg-pcapng.html#name-interface-statistics-block)\n\nSo we should add an ISB when converting from BLF to pcapng in order to keep that information, right?",
          "author": {
            "id": 18219493,
            "username": "giovanni.musto",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Giovanni Musto",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
